1. Variability

A supermarket has different numbers of customers at different times. Sometimes there are many customers and sometimes only a few. Some customers buy a few products and others buy many. This affects checkout lines and waiting times.

2. Complexity

A supermarket has many different tasks. Workers order products, fill shelves, help customers and handle payments. All these tasks need to work together.

3. Interconnectedness

Different things in a supermarket affect each other. If a product runs out, customers cannot buy it. The supermarket must order more products. If the delivery is late, the product is not available for customers.